# What goes into the source distribution.
#
# Only one instruction is needed, and it removes something rather than adding
# it.  Without this file setuptools applies its legacy default and sweeps
# `tests/test*.py` into the sdist -- but not `conftest.py`, which every one of
# those modules imports for `ATOL` and `traceless`.  The result was an sdist
# carrying 22 of the 41 files in `tests/` and a suite that could not even be
# collected: `pytest` in an unpacked sdist gave 13 collection errors, every one
# of them `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'conftest'`.  Someone who ran
# it would reasonably conclude the package was broken.
#
# Adding the missing files would not fix it, and that is why this prunes
# rather than completes.  Much of this suite tests the *repository* rather
# than the library: `test_file_tree.py` shells out to `git ls-files`,
# `test_documented_figures.py` reads `docs/source/*.rst`, and the
# notebook-count guard reads `notebooks/`.  None of that is in an sdist and
# there is no checkout to ask, so making the suite pass from a tarball would
# mean shipping essentially the whole repository and then weakening the guards
# most worth having.  The suite stays where it works, in a clone.
#
# Nothing else needs saying here: `docs/`, `notebooks/`, `examples/`, `img/`
# and `tools/` are already outside the default set and stay out.  The library,
# its metadata, the README and the licence are what an sdist is for, and both
# artefacts were checked to compute bit-identical probabilities to the source
# tree from a clean environment.
prune tests
